ust see the same level on five consecutive ticks", "state the longest bounce, in milliseconds, that this scheme tolerates"], "demoted": [], "fallback": false, "checked": true, "passed": true, "detail": "2 ask(s) anchored"}, "context": {"chars": 1726, "grounded": true, "asked": ["GPIO button debounce 1 kHz timer interrupt counter", "consecutive samples stable level debounce logic", "mechanical switch bounce duration specification firmware", "existing GPIO input interrupt handling pin configuration"], "rounds": 0, "lookup": null, "proven": [], "checked": true, "passed": true, "detail": "1726 characters of context assembled"}, "request_switches": {"skipped": false, "reason": "", "records": [{"switch": "needs_calculation", "probability": 0.9870789917903814, "choice": "yes", "mode": "live", "route": "yes", "item": "request"}], "route": {"needs_calculation": "yes"}, "cleared": [], "not_answering": []}, "implement": {"done": true, "green": true, "red_cause": null, "iteration": 1, "summary": "Configure a 1 kHz periodic timer interrupt (1 ms period) and have its ISR read the button GPIO once per tick. Keep a committed debounced level, a candidate level, and an unsigned counter. If the new sample equals the candidate, increment the counter (saturating at 5); if it differs, set the candidate to the new sample and set the counter to 1, so the current sample becomes the first of a new five-tick streak. When the counter reaches 5, copy the candidate to the debounced level and optionally set a flag or post an event for the main loop; the ISR never delays or busy-waits, and the main loop consumes only the committed level. The same logic handles press and release symmetrically.\n\nAt 1 kHz, one tick is 1 ms, so five consecutive matching ticks span a 5 ms debounce window. The scheme's debounce window is therefore 5 ms; equivalently, the longest bounce it is specified to tolerate is 5 ms (in practice, just under 5 ms for guaranteed filtering). A bounce of 5 ms or longer is not guaranteed to be filtered and can delay acceptance or, if it yields five identical samples, be mistaken for a valid level. The accepted level appears up to about 5 ms after the contact settles, so include that latency in the product's response budget and keep timer jitter and ISR latency small enough that the effective tick remains 1 ms.", "domain_concerns": [], "blocked_on": [], "not_stated": [], "results": [{"ask": 2, "value": 5.0, "unit": "ms"}]}, "blocked_check": {"blocked": [], "none_stated": [], "checked": true, "passed": true, "detail": "nothing blocked on"}, "answer_end": {"cut": false, "signs": [], "checked": true, "passed": true, "detail": "answer ends whole"}, "coverage": {"missed": [], "coverage": {"Describe, in two short paragraphs, how to debounce it with a 1 kHz timer interrupt and a counter that must see the same level on five consecutive ticks": 0.62, "state the longest bounce, in milliseconds, that this scheme tolerates": 0.5}, "addressed": 2, "shapes": {"Describe, in two short paragraphs, how to debounce it with a 1 kHz timer interrupt and a counter that must see the same level on five consecutive ticks": "presence", "state the longest bounce, in milliseconds, that this scheme tolerates": "presence"}, "abstained": [], "extractions": {"Describe, in two short paragraphs, how to debounce it with a 1 kHz timer interrupt and a counter that must see the same level on five consecutive ticks": {"terms": ["consecutive", "counter", "debounce", "describe", "five", "how", "interrupt", "khz", "level", "paragraphs", "same", "see", "short", "ticks", "timer", "two"], "forbidden_tokens": []}, "state the longest bounce, in milliseconds, that this scheme tolerates": {"terms": ["bounce", "longest", "milliseconds", "scheme", "state", "tolerates"], "forbidden_tokens": []}}, "checked": true, "passed": true, "detail": "all 2 measurable success criteria are addressed"}, "consistency": {"conflicts": [], "checked": true, "passed": true, "detail": "no contradicting values between r